@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia - the Hawaiian transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition, then the chant and the condition its
# efficacy is said to stand under, then the guard the tradition itself puts
# against that condition, then the house of the hula with its conduct, its
# cleansing and its graduation. The instance was registered in
# traditions/registry-outside-contact.ttl and is moved here now that passages
# have been read. Why its contact route is entered as documented, and why its
# identifier says cosmogony while the file carries chant mastery and hula,
# stands on the instance below, where it survives compilation.
#

pmt:HawaiianCosmogony a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hawaiian transmission"@en, "Hawaiische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Hawaiian transmission of a chanted genealogy of the world, of the mastery of chanting as an office with a stated condition of failure, and of the house of the hula with its conduct, its cleansing and its graduation."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: Beckwith's Kumulipo, chapters one, three and seven; Emerson's Unwritten Literature of Hawaii, chapters two, four and five. Not opened: Malo's chapters on necromancy and on the kuni rite, the Fornander volume on the order of sorcery, Kamakau's newspaper series, and Pukui's article of 1949 which Beckwith cites for the chanting rule. The reasons and the paths tried stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#ERNTE."@en ;
    skos:note "The identifier says cosmogony and the file carries chant mastery and hula. That is a defect and it is kept rather than corrected, because renaming a coined identifier is forbidden without compelling reason and RDF identity follows no redirect. The registration was written for the Kumulipo alone; the reading widened the tradition, not the transmission. The question and a recommendation stand at prima-materia#IDENT."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and it is documented twice over. First against the Maori transmission this graph already holds: both descend from one East Polynesian dispersal, dated archaeologically to the thirteenth century, so a correspondence between them is common inheritance and not two witnesses. Second against the Christian material: the mission arrives in 1820, the seminary at Lahainaluna is founded in 1831, and every work read here was written or printed after that by men trained in that school or by their successors. Absent was true of the islands before contact and false of the corpus this graph can cite."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Kumulipo in the Hawaiian text with Beckwith's edition; Malo, Moolelo Hawaii, in the Emerson translation and in the Langlas and Lyon edition; Emerson, Unwritten Literature of Hawaii; the Fornander Collection of Hawaiian Antiquities and Folk-Lore; Kamakau's series in Ke Au Okoa" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36" .

#
# The chant, the condition its efficacy stands under, and the guard the
# transmission itself puts against that condition
#

pmc:HawaiianChantExactness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The chant rendered without breath, mistake or hesitation"@en, "Der Gesang ohne Atemzug, Fehler und Zögern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The delivery a master of song is held to owe: no breath taken before the close of a phrase, no mistake, and no hesitation in pronouncing a word. The three are named together and on the same footing, so that pausing counts as failing rather than as taking care. The manner reported beside it is a tone almost on one note with every word enunciated distinctly."@en ;
    skos:note "Beckwith gives the rule in her own voice and names her authorities for it: Kamakau for the manner of delivery, and Pukui's article of 1949 for the practice. Neither was opened in this run; the article is behind a paywall and the newspaper series was not reachable. The node therefore stands on Beckwith and says so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianCosmogony ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36" .

pmc:HawaiianChantWelfare a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The good fortune of the person or family the chant honours"@en, "Das Wohlergehen der besungenen Person oder Familie"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the correct rendering is held to secure. The transmission places the outcome not with the reciter but with the party the chant is made for, which is the unusual part of this witness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianCosmogony ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35" .

pmc:HawaiianWelfarePresupposesExactChanting a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The good fortune of the honoured family presupposes a chant without fault"@en, "Das Wohlergehen der besungenen Familie setzt den fehlerlosen Gesang voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HawaiianChantWelfare ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HawaiianChantExactness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A breath taken before the close of a phrase, a mistake, or even hesitation in pronouncing a word was a sign of ill-luck to the person or family thus honoured. The harm is named, it is transferred, and it falls on the party who did not commit the fault." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HawaiianCosmogony ;
    skos:note "The transfer of the harm is what distinguishes this witness from the others the graph holds on the same rule. Where the Maori address is simply said to lose its efficacy and the Navajo ceremony is broken off, here the reciter is not the one who suffers. That makes the rule harder to test from inside the tradition, because the person who could report the fault and the person who bears the consequence are different people."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, p. 35" .

# --- ENDE ---
